{"title":"The Checklist It Can't Charm","markdown":{"headingText":"The Checklist It Can't Charm","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-checklist-cant-charm","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> Re-reading is not checking. A checklist that cannot run is a wish.\n\n**Lab companion:** [Lab 4, The Checklist It Can't Charm](https://michael-borck.github.io/programming-labs/lab-04-checklist-it-cant-charm/) — one running check; Chapter 9 assembles the full gate around it.\n\n## The Concept First\n\nLast chapter's spec defined done. This chapter makes done *checkable* — and the difference is the difference between a promise and a mechanism.\n\nHere is the mechanism's advantage. When the agent tells you \"done!\", you have three options: believe it (vibes), re-read the code yourself (a part-time job), or **run the definition of done** (seconds, and it works even when you are tired, busy, or charmed). Chapter 3's acceptance checklist was a list of sentences. This chapter promotes those sentences into checks — small, runnable, each answering one question about the product with PASS or FAIL.\n\nYou do not write the checks. You commission them, from the agent, from your spec — and then you do the one thing this book exists to teach: verify that the checks themselves are honest.\n\n## Understanding Through the Smoke Alarm\n\nNobody \"reads\" a smoke alarm. It is a checklist that runs itself, every second, and its entire value is that it fails loudly at 3 a.m. without asking permission. A smoke alarm that required you to remember to look at it would be decoration.\n\nMost people's AI projects have decoration where alarms should be: hopeful notes in a document (\"we should validate inputs\"), the agent's assurance (\"I've handled errors\"), a manual test done once, days ago. The slop comes in through exactly that gap.\n\n## Discovering Checks with Your AI Partner\n\n### Exploration 1: From Spec to Checks\n\nYour spec's \"Done means\" section converts almost mechanically:\n\n```text\nHere is my SPEC.md. Convert every item in \"Done means\" into a\nchecklist entry. Each entry must:\n- be a single run of something (open this page, run this command,\n  enter this input, compare to this expected output)\n- state the expected result exactly\n- be checkable by someone who has never seen our conversation\n\nOutput the checklist only. Do not fix or change the product.\n```\n\n### Exploration 2: Promote the Checks to Code\n\nThe most important checks should run without a human reading. For the tip calculator's arithmetic, that means tests — commissioned, again, from the spec:\n\n```text\nWrite test_checks.py using plain asserts. Each test corresponds to\none \"Done means\" item or one example from the spec. Include the\nrounding-residue rule and the invalid-input rule. Print one clear\nline per check: the rule tested and PASS/FAIL. No frameworks.\n```\n\n### Exploration 3: The Charm Test\n\nNow the meta-check, and it is the one that makes this chapter different from a testing tutorial:\n\n```text\nLook at the checks you just wrote. Which of them would still pass\nif the product were replaced with a hardcoded fake that prints the\nexpected answers? Rewrite any check that a fake could defeat so it\ntests the real behaviour instead.\n```\n\n::: {.callout-important title=\"The fake-detecting question\"}\nA check that a hardcoded fake could pass is checking nothing. \"Does the page show 24.00?\" can be defeated by printing 24.00. \"Does the page show the correct split for a 60 bill, 3 people, 10% tip — and for a 61.15 bill, 4 people, 15% tip?\" cannot be faked without doing the actual maths. When you commission checks, always ask the fake-detecting question.\n:::\n\n## From Concept to Code\n\nThe checks file, the kind of thing the agent produces from that prompt:\n\n```python\n# check_tips.py - runs the spec's rules and reports PASS/FAIL per rule.\nfrom tip_calculator import split_bill\n\ndef check(name, condition):\n    print((\"PASS \" if condition else \"FAIL \"), name)\n    return condition\n\nresults = [\n    check(\"60 / 3 people / 10% splits to 22.00 each\",\n          split_bill(60, 3, 10) == [22.00, 22.00, 22.00]),\n    check(\"Rounding residue goes to the first person\",\n          split_bill(61.15, 4, 15)[0] > split_bill(61.15, 4, 15)[1]),\n    check(\"Zero bill rejected with a clear message\",\n          split_bill(0, 2, 10) == \"Error: bill must be > 0\"),\n    check(\"Party size above 20 rejected\",\n          split_bill(60, 21, 10) == \"Error: party size is 1 to 20\"),\n]\n\nprint(\"PASSED\" if all(results) else \"SOME CHECKS FAILED\",\n      f\"({sum(results)}/{len(results)})\")\n```\n\n::: {.callout-warning title=\"Checks need care too\"}\nThe agent's checks can be wrong in the agent's favour — too few cases, a fake-defeatable comparison, an expected value copied from the program's output instead of the spec. Audit the checks against the spec exactly as fiercely as you audit the product. Chapter 9 turns this suspicion into a policy.\n:::\n\n## Business Connection\n\nA client who can run your acceptance checklist owns the verification themselves — your honesty stops being the requirement. That is why businesses pay premiums for suppliers who arrive with runable evidence: it converts trust from a feeling into a procedure. Internally, the same list settles the argument about whether the work is finished before the argument starts.\n\nThis is also the seed of the entire quality industry: lint rules, test suites, coverage floors — Chapter 9's full gate is this chapter's checklist with a career. Start the habit small and the scale arrives free.\n\n## Practice Exercises\n\n**Exercise 5.1 (Level 1).** Convert your Chapter 3 acceptance checklist into checks; run them; record the score honestly.\n\n**Exercise 5.2 (Level 2).** Run the charm test on your own checks. How many could a hardcoded fake defeat? Rewrite them.\n\n**Exercise 5.3 (Level 3).** Commission the same checks from a *fresh* agent, using only the spec. Diff the two checklists — the differences are spec gaps.\n\n**Exercise 5.4 (Level 4).** Write one check that the current agent genuinely cannot satisfy, and specify what the product must do to pass it.\n\n**Exercise 5.5 (Level 5).** Design the acceptance checklist for this book's next chapter, before reading it. After reading, grade your design.\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n- Spec defines done; checks make done enforceable\n- Runnable beats readable: a check works on your worst day\n- The fake-detecting question separates real checks from decoration\n- Commission the checks from the spec — then audit the checker\n\n## Reflection\n\nBefore moving on, ensure you:\n\n- [ ] Have a checks file in the repository, committed\n- [ ] Ran the fake-detecting question on at least one check\n- [ ] Fixed a product defect the checks exposed\n- [ ] Never marked a check PASS without running it\n\n## Your Learning Journal\n\n1. What did your first honest run of the checks reveal?\n2. Which was harder to write — the product or the check? What does that tell you?\n3. What in your non-work life deserves a runnable checklist?\n\n## Next Steps\n\nThe checks verify the present. The next chapter protects the past: version control as the department's flight recorder — small commits, fearless branches, and the ability to undo anything.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"outp